From c6beabafbbcfb61cb432879e3f967ce842ba2821 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:43:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Aurora: Smooth Motion pairs clouds and wave crests by
 their own vertices

With Smooth Motion, single clouds went see-through for an in-between frame
or three every few seconds, still or moving, and the small wave crests on a
calm sea flickered (chrissotraidis/bluewake#136). Both are drawn as copies
of one shape with the view matrix and their own world positions
(d_kankyo_rain's drawVrkumo and drawWave), so every copy has the same block
and stands where the camera's motion carries any of them. Copies were then
paired by their place in the list, which is the game's slots with the
invisible ones left out: when one cloud faded out or came into view, every
cloud after it was paired with its neighbour, and its in-between colours
were blended toward the neighbour's alpha. The crests, up to 300 that come
and go all the time, were paired that way most of the time.

Such a copy now keeps the centre of its vertices, and is paired with the
copy of the frame before whose centre is nearest, each once; a copy with
none within 1000 units is new and drawn as it is. Found in a per-draw trace
on Windows: cloud alpha jumps only where the number of clouds changed, each
paired with the same place in the list.

The game draws each cloud twice with one shape, once with alpha 1 for every
cloud and then with its own, the two centres some 15 units apart while the
sky moves up to 130 units a frame as the title screen's camera pans. The
nearest centre alone paired a cloud with its other copy now and then (the
cloud below the title's Z flickered), so a copy's colours count as well: one
level of colour difference weighs as one unit of distance. On the title pan,
in-between frames with a cloud brighter or darker than both real frames went
from 384 to 7 (the 7 are the logo coming in), and copies whose alpha jumped
by more than 100 from 302 to none.
