From be07338a68cf4ca85a72a0fc03a54a3aab720283 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 18:25:17 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Aurora: Smooth Motion follows the rings and wake Link
 leaves as he swims

With Smooth Motion, the water around a swimming Link stepped at 30 FPS or
jumped (chrissotraidis/bluewake#136, a comment there):

- A ripple's rings are flat quads that grow every frame. Their in-between
  frames kept the next frame's vertices, and the rings were paired by their
  place in the list, which shifts as a ring comes and goes: a ring took its
  neighbour's size for a frame. They are paired by their vertices' centre now
  (as the clouds are) with their size counting too, and their vertices are
  blended one by one.
- A copy pairs only with one less than half its size away (true pairs move
  some 5 units a frame). A new ring of the trail behind Link, drawn first, had
  taken the last one born, and each ring after it the one behind it: the whole
  trail went back a ring each time one was born. A copy with none near enough
  is new, and stands where the camera's motion carries it from.
- The wake is two strips through its emitter's particles that grow by one a
  frame (52, 54, 56 vertices...), keyed by their vertex count, so a new key
  each frame and no counterpart. Such a strip is keyed without its count, and
  its vertices are paired with the frame before's at the shift (up to two
  particles either way) that lines its particles up; a new particle grows out
  of the end.
- A draw with more than eight vertices is not one particle's and takes no
  particle tag. The wind's lines in the sky, drawn by their emitter's callback
  after its last particle, found that particle's tag, a new one every frame.

Checked on Windows: a scripted swim (save 1, jump out of the boat, swim)
dumped real and in-between frames, and I swam and sailed in a local build
that dumps the next frames on a key. Around Link, in-between frames with more
than 200 pixels brighter or darker than both real frames went from 222 to 32
of 480, and the trail's rings each keep their counterpart at every birth. The
title pan and a sail with the camera turning pair their clouds as before.

frame_interp_test checks a ripple's rings pairing with their own in either
order, a ring born away from them drawn as it is, and a trail growing by a
particle at its start. Its untagged-shape case (the boat's shadow on the sea's
triangles) is a triangle list now, as the game draws it: an untagged strip
with its own positions is blended vertex by vertex.
